WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.940
It’s been an amazing 2017
for League of Legends,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.180
with some great plays and even greater stories.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.820
So in honor of some of the best
player storylines of the year,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.820
we put together the Top 10.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.460
But before we get into that, be sure to check out thescoreesportsbuilds.com

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.960
to help take your League skills to the next level.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.160
And now for our list of the Top 10 best player storylines of the year.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.780
Kicking off our list is the rookie superstar MikeYeung,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.520
who helped North America win their Rift Rivals showdown against EU

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.020
and placed 10th with Phoenix1 in the summer split.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.840
MikeYeung exploded onto the NA scene this summer,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.420
with impressive performances on carry junglers that quickly earned him a fan following.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.300
And despite that disappointing 10th place
finish,

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.420
MikeYeung earned Rookie of the Split for his stellar play,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.460
and ended the year off
by signing with TSM for 2018.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:01:00.220
[MikeYeung talking]

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.560
Though he is still relatively inexperienced,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.240
MikeYeung will definitely be a player to watch heading into next year.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:23.780
[Caster audio]

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.600
Uzi, who comes in at ninth on our list,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.980
helped Royal Never Give Up win the Spring Split in the LPL,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.600
come second in the summer and place
in the Top 4 at the world championship.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.420
He is also China’s All-Star AD carry.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.800
One of the LPL’s most veteran players,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.840
Uzi was a key figure in China’s move toward all-Chinese rosters in 2017,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.520
and RNG were one of its most successful examples.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:59.040
[Caster audio]

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.500
Uzi is a part of China’s League of Legends
history.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.400
And thanks to his play this year,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.790
he is also a part of its future.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:10.350
With more Chinese teams looking to follow
the examples of squads like Royal and OMG,

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.860
we’re excited to see where the region’s
domestic talent can go.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:32.550
[Caster audio]

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.340
In eighth is IgNar who placed fourth with
Misfits in the Spring Split

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.860
and second in the summer.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.220
He is also Europe’s All-Star support.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.750
As a relatively new team, Misfits put up strong
results in 2017.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.440
But besides making it to the summer final
against G2,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.100
their story really got interesting at the World Championship.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:03:05.459
[Caster audio]

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:09.740
Thanks in large part to IgNar’s innovative
support play on Leona and Blitzcrank,

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.100
the plucky Misfits took SKT to five games

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.340
and came within inches of knocking the defending champions out of the tournament.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:26.310
[Caster audio]

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.520
It was further than anyone expected Misfits
to go.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.420
And though SKT won in the end,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.420
it was one of the most exciting series of the year.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:48.240
[Daku talking]

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.980
Coming in at number seven,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.540
Xmithie placed 5th-6th in the Spring Split with CLG,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.020
second with Immortals in the Summer Split

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.900
and attended the World Championship.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:02.160
Xmithie’s story in 2017 really comes down
to the summer split.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:13.000
[Caster audio]

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.940
He came into an Immortals roster that struggled
to find its footing in the Spring Split,

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.020
placing 7th, and he changed everything.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.780
After CLG and Immortals swapped junglers. Dardoch
and Xmithie,

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:28.040
all eyes were on a flashy, seemingly empowered, CLG roster.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.200
But Xmithie smashed those expectations.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.920
[Caster audio]

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.680
He flipped the script on an Immortals squad
the community had largely written off.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.900
His stabilizing presence in the jungle was
a huge factor

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.960
in Immortals making it to the
 summer finals in North America.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.920
And although the team did not find much success at Worlds,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.920
it probably wouldn’t have made it there at all without Xmithie.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:05:01.640
[Caster audio]

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.560
Number six on our list is Europe’s veteran
AD carry, Rekkles.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.260
Fnatic’s roster of relatively untested players

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.240
put up surprisingly consistent results in the EU LCS this year,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.540
placing third in both splits.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.520
And thanks to his performance this summer,
Rekkles was named the MVP.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.740
But it was at Worlds that Rekkles led the
team to its most surprising result.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:32.600
[Caster audio]

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.840
After an 0-3 start to the group stage,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.260
Fnatic made it further than anyone could have expected.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:44.580
[Caster audio]

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.060
But after being handed a rough defeat by Royal
Never Give Up in the quarterfinals,

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.440
Rekkles was devastated.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.380
He’d taken his team further than anyone
expected,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.560
but it wasn’t enough to make a deeper run at Worlds.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.504
That said, Rekkles has re-signed with Fnatic
for three more years,

00:06:01.504 --> 00:06:05.240
and we’re excited to see what he can do in 2018.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:10.440
[Caster audio]

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.160
Number five on our list is Doublelift.

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:16.180
Doublelift’s 2017 started with a lull

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.120
as the veteran AD carry took a step back from the scene

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.340
after a grueling end to 2016.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:26.380
But his passion for the competitive scene
drove him back to the Rift sooner than expected.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:31.040
Doublelift subbed in for Team
Liquid to help that team salvage a tough Spring Split.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.820
[Caster audio]

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:36.920
He returned to TSM for the summer and helped
the team win another domestic title,

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.800
though they went on to struggle at Worlds.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.420
But the ending to his year might be the most
significant,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.640
with Doublelift returning to Team Liquid in earnest

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.020
ahead of the 2018 Spring Split.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.640
We're excited to see what seems like a friendly rivalry bloom.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.180
[Doublelift talking]

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.460
Number four on our list is Crown,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.140
who placed third in the Spring Split and fourth in the summer.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.100
But Crown’s biggest achievement of the year,

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.740
his win at the 2017 World Championship,

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.080
came after a year of personal struggles for the mid laner.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.080
Crown might not have been the strongest mid
laner in Korea this year,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.500
but along with his teammates on Samsung,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:28.640
he overcame personal adversity and brought it all together when it mattered most.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:35.360
Crown’s story is proof that persistence
and hard work can take you all the way.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:42.860
[Caster audio]

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.520
Number three on our list,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.660
Bdd became the starting mid laner

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.640
on a Longzhu roster that had a terrible Spring Split.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.660
But with the help of their summer roster,

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.500
Longzhu brought it back to win the split.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:08:02.820
[Caster audio]

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.040
And though that had a lot to do with Pray
and Gorilla’s leadership,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.920
Bdd was a huge factor in their summer run,

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.100
going toe-to-toe with the likes of Faker,

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.060
and finally living up to his enormous potential.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.360
Coming in at number two is Ambition,

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.080
who was also part of Samsung’s third place Spring Split

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.300
and fourth place summer rosters.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.640
But at Worlds, Ambition led his team to the
Summoner’s Cup,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.280
with a huge 3-0 win over SKT in the finals.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:46.760
[Caster audio]

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:51.020
But for Ambition, the win was a story four
years in the making.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:55.740
[Caster audio]

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.640
After what seems like a lifetime in esports,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.700
Ambition finally got his revenge on Faker.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:05.880
And what better way to do it than on League’s
biggest stage?

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:20.860
And finally, at number one,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.399
who else could it be but Faker?

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:26.240
Faker’s 2017 started off strong

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.340
with another domestic title
and a win at the Mid-Season Invitational,

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.160
but in the back half of the year,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.400
the world’s most successful player
and most successful team stumbled.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:42.620
[Caster audio]

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:47.280
Faker’s phenomenal play in 2017 cemented
him as the best player in the world.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:54.800
[Caster audio]

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.260
But it was his defeat at the end of the year that
showed us a new side of Faker.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.000
Not satisfied with his many accolades,

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.760
Faker was devastated by his loss.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.999
He’ll enter 2018 hungry, with something
to prove once again.

00:10:07.999 --> 00:10:10.640
And we’re excited to see where he goes.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:22.960
[Caster audio]

